WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.534
On a Wednesday in 1932, a long line suddenly forms outside a bank.

2
00:00:05.034 --> 00:00:08.237
The bank manager is puzzled: the bank’s finances still seem sound.

3
00:00:08.737 --> 00:00:12.498
But a rumor says it will fail. The more people believe it, the longer the withdrawal queue grows.

4
00:00:12.998 --> 00:00:15.149
By Thursday, it really has failed.

5
00:00:16.049 --> 00:00:20.729
This Last National Bank is a story sociologist Robert Merton invented in 1948.

6
00:00:21.229 --> 00:00:25.106
He used it to explain a concept: the self-fulfilling prophecy.

7
00:00:26.006 --> 00:00:31.564
An initially false belief brings about new actions that eventually make it true.

8
00:00:32.064 --> 00:00:36.351
For example, a student convinced of failing an exam becomes so preoccupied with worry that they actually fail.

9
00:00:37.251 --> 00:00:47.769
Merton’s main concern was racial prejudice in the US: unions excluded Black workers, who then filled jobs during strikes, reinforcing the excuse for excluding them.

10
00:00:48.669 --> 00:00:53.254
How can the cycle be broken? He placed less hope in persuasion and propaganda, and more in institutions.

11
00:00:53.754 --> 00:00:58.833
In the 1920s, over six hundred US banks suspended operations each year on average; around the Great Depression, over two thousand did.

12
00:00:59.333 --> 00:01:04.552
In the twelve years after deposit insurance and other banking legislation, the annual average was twenty-eight.

13
00:01:05.052 --> 00:01:09.818
His point was that depositors stopped rushing to withdraw because institutions removed their reason for panic.

14
00:01:10.718 --> 00:01:17.130
But this is an essay using figures to illustrate an argument. He also said panic might not have been fully eliminated.

15
00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:23.565
Have you seen something happen because everyone believed it would? Share your thoughts.

16
00:01:24.065 --> 00:01:25.894
What people believe can also change what happens.
